Pendulum oscillation period is related to the length of the pendulum, $T \sim \sqrt{L}$. The change in temperature of 10 degrees will cause the length to increase to $L(1+\Delta T\alpha)$ , where $\alpha$ is the coefficient of linear expansion. Therefore the new period will be $(1+\Delta T\alpha)^{1/2} T$. The percentage decrease in period is thus about .5% (10 degrees times 16X10^-6 degrees^-1, divided by 2). This is less than one second per day, about .6s. In a week, it would lose about 4.2 seconds. Clearly the correct choice is (G). That is the answer.
